10	/**
11	 * LOD frustum growth, step schedule, and the forward N/E/D occupancy map used at an open-cell
12	 * exit.
13	 *
14	 * <p>Footprint width {@code = }{@link #BASE_FOOTPRINT}{@code + growthPerBlock * distance}, where
15	 * {@code growthPerBlock} comes from {@link #growthPerBlock(int)} — never a hardcoded constant, since
16	 * the correct rate depends on how many rays share the sphere (see below). Step sizes follow
17	 * {@code 1,1,2,2,4,4,8,8,16,16} then stay capped at {@link #MAX_STEP}.
18	 *
19	 * <p>{@code growthPerBlock} is derived, not tuned by hand: {@code nRays} beams are spread over the
20	 * full sphere ({@code 4π} steradians, see {@code Engine.updateRays}' golden-angle spiral, which
21	 * is equal-area so every beam owns on average {@code 4π/nRays} steradians regardless of it being
22	 * one continuous spiral rather than a per-axis sweep). Modeling each beam's cross-section as a
23	 * square pyramidal frustum (matching the square N/E/D octree grid, not a circular cone) with equal
24	 * half-angle {@code θ} on both axes gives solid angle {@code Ω = 4*asin(sin²θ)}; setting
25	 * {@code Ω = 4π/nRays} and solving for the footprint's linear diameter-growth-per-block
26	 * ({@code 2*tanθ}) yields {@link #growthPerBlock(int)}. Callers must fetch this once from
27	 * {@code PrecomputedConfig.pConfig.frustumGrowthPerBlock} (cached whenever {@code nRays} changes)
28	 * rather than calling {@link #growthPerBlock(int)} per-cast — it involves asin/sin/tan and has no
29	 * business running inside the raycast hot path.
30	 *
31	 * <p>N/E/D axes come from the next two octant-sized DDA boundaries the ray will strike (signed
32	 * travel directions), not from “face plane + dominant tangent”. The first boundary is the exit
33	 * just resolved; the second is projected by continuing Amanatides–Woo arithmetic one same-size
34	 * cell forward <em>without</em> fetching that neighbor from the octree. If that projected face
35	 * shares the first face's axis, the exit is an ordinary single-axis DDA step (no N/E/D survey).
36	 * Otherwise {@code N} is the first axis, {@code E} the second, and {@code D = N+E}. Trailing cells
37	 * are never surveyed. Four maps (same heading):
38	 * <pre>
39	 *   N | D          1 CORNER  X X / O X  reflect, walk to the shared vertex
40	 *  ---+---         2 GAP     X O / O O  pass through E/D
41	 *   H | E          3 SPLIT   X O / O X  both (1) and (2) as child beams
42	 *                  4 FACE    X X / O O  walk to D–E-only vertex, then reflect
43	 * </pre>
44	 */
45	public final class FrustumLod {
46	    private FrustumLod() {}
47	
48	    public static final double BASE_FOOTPRINT = 1.0;
49	    public static final int MAX_STEP = 16;
50	
51	    /**
52	     * Linear footprint-diameter growth per block of travel for {@code nRays} beams tiling the full
53	     * sphere with no gaps (square-pyramidal-frustum model). See the class doc for the derivation.
54	     * Pure math — cheap enough on its own, but callers should still read the cached
55	     * {@code PrecomputedConfig.pConfig.frustumGrowthPerBlock} instead of calling this per-cast; it
56	     * only needs recomputing when {@code nRays} (a config value) changes.
57	     */
58	    public static double growthPerBlock(int nRays) {
59	        double n = Math.max(1, nRays);
60	        double solidAnglePerRay = 4.0 * Math.PI / n;
61	        double halfAngle = Math.asin(Math.sqrt(Math.sin(solidAnglePerRay / 4.0)));
62	        return 2.0 * Math.tan(halfAngle);
63	    }
64	
65	    static final double AXIS_EPS = 1e-9;
